From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alsey Coleman Miller <alseycmiller@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Define OPENSSL_64_BIT on powerpc64le

BoringSSL's target detection does not know PowerPC (upstream removed
ppc64le support), so on powerpc64le neither OPENSSL_32_BIT nor
OPENSSL_64_BIT gets defined and every header errors out with
"Must define either OPENSSL_32_BIT or OPENSSL_64_BIT".

powerpc64le is a standard 64-bit two's-complement little-endian
architecture, which is exactly what the portable C code supports (see the
comment above the #error). Add a branch defining OPENSSL_64_BIT; it is
used together with the OPENSSL_NO_ASM build from 0001-*.patch. The
OPENSSL_PPC64LE macro is deliberately not defined so no stale
arch-specific paths are enabled.
---
 Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/include/CCryptoBoringSSL_target.h | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/include/CCryptoBoringSSL_target.h
+++ b/Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/include/CCryptoBoringSSL_target.h
@@ -54,6 +54,12 @@
 #define OPENSSL_32_BIT
 #elif defined(__myriad2__)
 #define OPENSSL_32_BIT
+#elif defined(__powerpc64__) && defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && \
+    __BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__
+// powerpc64le is not an officially supported BoringSSL target, but it is a
+// standard 64-bit two's-complement little-endian architecture, which the
+// portable C code supports (used with OPENSSL_NO_ASM by the Buildroot build).
+#define OPENSSL_64_BIT
 #else
 // The list above enumerates the platforms that BoringSSL supports. For these
 // platforms we keep a reasonable bar of not breaking them: automated test
